that the house go into committee at the next sitting to consider the following resolution, which has been recommended to the house by His Excellency:
That it is expedient to introduce a measure for the further improvement of housing and living conditions and for that purpose:
(a)	to provide for payments of contributions to municipalities in order to assist in the clearance, replanning, rehabilitation and modernization of
blighted areas, up to twenty-five million dollars and such other amounts as may be subsequently authorized;
(b)	to increase the amount of a home improvement loan that may be guaranteed under part IV of the National Housing Act, 1954; and to increase the aggregate amount of home improvement loans that may be guaranteed, from one hundred and twenty-five million dollars to two hundred million dollars;
(c)	to increase the aggregate amount of all loans in respect of which insurance policies may be issued under part I of the act from two billion dollars to four billion dollars;
(d)	to provide five million dollars out of which to pay expenses of Central Mortgage and Housing Corporation incurred in carrying out its responsibilities under part V of the act relating to housing research and community planning;
(e)	to provide fifty million dollars for federal-provincial land assembly and housing projects; and
(f)	to provide for a number of administrative and other changes deemed necessary for the more efficient administration of the act.

I am not going to take up any more of the time of the house. The hon. member for Digby-Annapolis-Kings spoke about an organization that was formed. We are told to help ourselves. We have never tried to shirk helping ourselves. I cite the effort at Beechwood to develop power. Is that not a great step toward helping ourselves? We should like to get a little bit more help from others. This organization which was mentioned a few moments ago and which has been set up in the Atlantic provinces is called the Atlantic provinces economic council. It was formed for no other purpose than to help ourselves. I should like to read from one of their by-laws under the heading of "Membership":
1.	The membership shall consist of persons from the Atlantic provinces representing such interests as agriculture, construction, fisheries, labour, mining, manufacturing, banking and investments, insurance, transportation, public utilities, wholesale and retail trade, universities, tourist business, professions and other lines of business prominent in the said provinces.
2.	It shall be the privilege of any member or members of the council to withdraw therefrom at any time upon giving to the secretary thereof written notice of their intention so to do at least seven days prior to the date fixed for the annual meeting.
Then under the heading "Objects and Purposes" we find the following:
The objects and purposes of the Atlantic provinces economic council shall be:
I.	To survey and study the advantages and difficulties, including alternate transportation, under which the population of the said Atlantic provinces is operating for the purpose of :
1.	Discovering the forces which should make for economic growth and stability within the said provinces;

2.	Making recommendations concerning methods calculated to ensure the economic future of the said provinces;
3.	Promoting or opposing legislative and other measures affecting trade, commerce, manufacture and industrial development of the said provinces.
II. To stimulate;
(1)	Concrete expression as to matters vital to the economic welfare of the Atlantic provinces among organizations participating in its membership;
(2)	United action among the said provinces;
(3)	The promotion of the Atlantic provinces' agriculture, commerce, fisheries, industry, public utilities, transportation and other matters of common concern.
In this connection I might say that this council which has been formed in the Atlantic provinces is based on similar councils which were formed in the New England states, and which were to a great extent responsible for the interest of the federal government and the obtaining of industries in that section of the country.
In closing I wish to repeat that there is a responsibility on the federal government. The matter of freight rates cannot be dealt with by the province of New Brunswick or by the people of New Brunswick. The matter of tariffs cannot be dealt with by them. The matter of using our raw material in this country and prohibiting it from going outside to be processed, fabricated and manufactured cannot be dealt with by the people of the small provinces. There is where the federal government can help and there, I say, is the responsibility which rests upon the federal government in this country.

Tomorrow and Wednesday we shall take the resolution to set up the committee on railways and shipping owned and operated by the government; then the resolution to set up the federal district committee;

then the one with respect to the Criminal Code, followed then by the resolution in my name with respect to the Industrial Development Bank Act, and then second reading of the Small Loans Act. I should add that the latter two bills will be going to the banking and commerce committee after second reading.
Business of the House
